{"step_index":438,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T12:52:46Z","thinking":"**Summarizing Findings and Configurations**\n\nFindings are being compiled, encompassing identified issues and applied configurations. The summary will explain the rationale behind each configuration change. The chain's current operational status will be included in the summary.\n\n\n**Creating Comprehensive Report Artifact**\n\nThe request calls for a detailed report, including findings, configurations, and status. An artifact will be generated, as it aligns with instructions for comprehensive summaries. This artifact will comprehensively address the user's specific request. The artifact directory is being accessed to manage the file creation.\n\n\n**Analyzing Certificate Verification Failure**\n\nThe initial investigation confirmed network connectivity and the presence of a TLS-enabled Tomcat server. Strict TLS verification within the web application is likely failing because the expected certificate is not trusted.  Bypassing TLS verification in CLI tests succeeds, confirming underlying connectivity. The mismatch in trust is the central focus now.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Certificate Validation Failure**\n\nThe identified root cause is a Subject Alternative Name (SAN) mismatch during TLS verification. The certificate issued for the Nginx reverse proxy `th2-revproxyp-fe1` lacks `map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr`. This results in SSL verification failures when PHP cURL attempts to connect.\n\n**Applying Reverse Proxy Configuration**\n\nThe Nginx configuration was updated to address the SAN mismatch by explicitly including `map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr` and `extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr` within the `server_name` directive. `proxy_ssl_verify off;` was used temporarily due to the self-signed certificate, with `proxy_pass` set to the correct backend and port. HTTP headers were set to preserve the correct port and protocol for the backend service.\n\n\n**Configuring Loopback Alias and Host Resolution**\n\nA loopback alias was configured to accept local packets addressed to a public IP, avoiding external routing. The `/etc/hosts` file on the backend was updated to resolve the public FQDN directly to the reverse proxy's internal DMZ IP, bypassing firewall NAT limitations.\n\n**Assessing Chain's Operational Status**\n\nThe system data flow was examined, highlighting how external browsers access the map service. The central backend PHP path was verified, confirming network and proxy functionality. The reverse proxy and backend host resolution are active.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":"{\"RequestFeedback\":false,\"Summary\":\"Comprehensive architecture and status report for Centreon Map integration via reverse proxy th2-revproxyp-fe1 and Central th2-centprop-be1. Details findings (502 root cause, hairpin NAT limitations, SSL SAN validation failure), all applied configurations with technical rationales, end-to-end traffic flow diagrams, and exact remediation steps.\",\"UserFacing\":true}","CodeContent":"\"# Centreon Map Integration & Reverse Proxy Chain Report\\n\\n## 1. Executive Summary\\n\\nThis report documents the diagnostic findings, configuration changes, and operational status of the Centreon Map reverse proxy chain linking **Centreon Web** (`extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:443`), **Nginx Reverse Proxy** (`th2-revproxyp-fe1`), and **Centreon Map Server** (`172.30.198.50:9443`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Key Findings & Root Cause Analysis\\n\\n### A. Initial 502 Bad Gateway on Port 9443\\n* **Observation**: Requests to `https://<reverse-proxy>:9443/` returned `502 Bad Gateway`.\\n* **Root Cause**: The original Nginx configuration defined `proxy_pass http://172.30.198.50;` (plain HTTP port 80). Port 80 on `172.30.198.50` was completely closed (`Connection refused`). Centreon Map is powered by Apache Tomcat listening strictly on **HTTPS port 9443** with TLS.\\n\\n### B. Firewall Hairpin NAT Absence\\n* **Observation**: Backend `th2-centprop-be1` (`172.30.198.49`) could not reach public IP `109.62.64.18:9443`.\\n* **Root Cause**: Synchronized packet captures on `th2-revproxyp-fe1` showed `0 packets` arriving when requests were directed to `109.62.64.18`. The perimeter firewall gateway (`172.30.198.54`) performs Destination NAT (DNAT) exclusively for incoming WAN traffic. It does not perform NAT loopback / Hairpin NAT for internal DMZ traffic targeting its own public VIP.\\n\\n### C. Subnet Separation & Routing Constraints\\n* **Observation**: Attempting `ip route add 109.62.64.18/32 via 172.30.198.33` on `th2-centprop-be1` failed with `Error: Nexthop has invalid gateway`.\\n* **Root Cause**: Central (`172.30.198.49`) is on subnet `172.30.198.48/29` while the reverse proxy (`172.30.198.33`) is on `172.30.198.32/29`. A host cannot use an IP outside its own broadcast subnet as a direct next-hop gateway without layer-3 routing via `.54`.\\n\\n### D. UI \\\"Test Connection to Server\\\" Button Failure\\n* **Observation**: In Centreon Web (`Administration > Extensions > Options`), the \\\"Test connection to server\\\" button failed, while CLI `curl\n<truncated 6585 bytes>","Description":"\"Generated comprehensive findings and status report artifact for the Centreon Map reverse proxy chain integration.\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/centreon-map-chain-report.md\"","toolAction":"\"Creating report artifact\"","toolSummary":"\"Centreon Map Chain Report\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
