{"step_index":481,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T13:07:19Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-10T17:07:19+04:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-10T17:07:19+04:00\nFile Path: `file:///home/koby/.ansible/playbooks/group_vars/all/vault.yml.example`\nTotal Lines: 33\nTotal Bytes: 1441\nShowing lines 1 to 33\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: # MODÈLE — ne pas utiliser tel quel, ce fichier n'est PAS chiffré.\n3: #\n4: # Créez le vrai fichier chiffré avec :\n5: #   ansible-vault create group_vars/all/vault.yml\n6: # (ou éditez-le ensuite avec : ansible-vault edit group_vars/all/vault.yml)\n7: # puis copiez cette structure dedans en remplaçant les valeurs.\n8: #\n9: # Ne mettez jamais ces secrets en clair ailleurs dans le dépôt.\n10: \n11: # --- Backend rfc2136 (ns1 / BIND) ---\n12: vault_rfc2136_server: \"203.0.113.10\"        # IP de ns1\n13: vault_rfc2136_port: 53\n14: vault_rfc2136_tsig_key_name: \"acme-key.\"\n15: vault_rfc2136_tsig_secret: \"BASE64_TSIG_SECRET_ICI\"\n16: vault_rfc2136_tsig_algorithm: \"HMAC-SHA512\"\n17: \n18: # --- Backend powerdns (dnsp-mst) ---\n19: vault_powerdns_api_url: \"https://dnsp-mst.example.lan/api/v1\"\n20: vault_powerdns_api_key: \"CLE_API_POWERDNS_ICI\"\n21: vault_powerdns_dns_host: \"dnsp-mst.example.lan\"   # interrogé directement pour vérifier la propagation\n22: \n23: # --- Backend ssh_zonefile (ex. ns1 — pas de RFC2136, édition via SSH) ---\n24: # vault_ns1_ssh_alias doit correspondre à un \"Host\" défini dans le\n25: # ~/.ssh/config du nœud de contrôle (clé RSA déployée, options de\n26: # compatibilité legacy réglées une fois là — voir Host ns1 dans ~/.ssh/config).\n27: vault_ns1_ssh_alias: \"ns1\"\n28: vault_ns1_dns_host: \"172.16.75.250\"\n29: vault_ns1_sudo_password: \"MOT_DE_PASSE_SUDO_ADMIN_NS1_ICI\"\n30: \n31: # --- Compte email pour les notifications d'expiration Let's Encrypt ---\n32: vault_letsencrypt_email: \"admin@outremer-telecom.fr\"\n33: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":482,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T13:07:19Z","content":"Yes, I checked [`~/.ansible/`](file:///home/koby/.ansible). You have a dedicated playbook suite specifically designed for this:\n\n**[`~/.ansible/playbooks/issue-deploy-certs.yml`](file:///home/koby/.ansible/playbooks/issue-deploy-certs.yml)**\n\n---\n\n### How This Playbook Works\n\nUnlike standard Certbot (which uses HTTP-01 and requires port 80 traffic to hit the machine), this playbook uses **DNS-01 ACME challenges**:\n\n1. **Runs locally on your Debian Trixie machine (`hosts: localhost`)**:\n   * It does **not** need any incoming HTTP traffic.\n   * It talks directly to your DNS server to publish the `_acme-challenge` TXT record.\n   * Let's Encrypt verifies the DNS TXT record and issues the certificate directly to your local `/etc/letsencrypt/live/`.\n\n2. **Automated Remote Deployment**:\n   * Once issued locally, it uses [`playbooks/tasks/deploy_and_reload_cert.yml`](file:///home/koby/.ansible/playbooks/tasks/deploy_and_reload_cert.yml) to copy `fullchain.pem` and `privkey.pem` to the target host (e.g. `th2-revproxyp-fe1`).\n   * It only reloads Nginx/Apache if the certificate serial number actually changed.\n\n---\n\n### Supported DNS Backends\n\nConfigured in [`~/.ansible/playbooks/group_vars/all/certificates.yml`](file:///home/koby/.ansible/playbooks/group_vars/all/certificates.yml):\n\n| Backend | Protocol | Used For |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`rfc2136`** | BIND with TSIG key via `certbot-dns-rfc2136` | `outremer-telecom.fr` |\n| **`powerdns`** | PowerDNS REST API (`th2-dnsp-mst1`) via auth hooks | `sfrcaraibe.fr`, `redcaraibe.fr` |\n| **`ssh_zonefile`** | SSH edit of BIND zone file on `ns1` (`acme-zone-update.sh`) | `only.fr` |\n\n---\n\n### How to Use It for `map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr`\n\n#### Option A: Declare in Catalogue\nAdd the entry in [`~/.ansible/playbooks/group_vars/all/certificates.yml`](file:///home/koby/.ansible/playbooks/group_vars/all/certificates.yml):\n\n```yaml\n  - domain: map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n    extra_sans: []\n    dns_backend: rfc2136\n    target_host: 172.30.198.33\n    webserver: nginx\n    service_name: nginx\n    cert_dest_dir: /etc/nginx/ssl/map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n```\n\nThen run from your Debian machine:\n```bash\ncd ~/.ansible\n# 1. Test run in STAGING (safety default)\nansible-playbook playbooks/issue-deploy-certs.yml --ask-vault-pass \\\n  -e certs_filter=map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n\n# 2. Production run\nansible-playbook playbooks/issue-deploy-certs.yml --ask-vault-pass \\\n  -e certs_filter=map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr \\\n  -e letsencrypt_staging=false\n```\n\n#### Option B: Mode Ad-hoc (Single Command)\n```bash\ncd ~/.ansible\nansible-playbook playbooks/issue-deploy-certs.yml --ask-vault-pass --ask-become-pass \\\n  --tags issue \\\n  -e cert_domain=map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr \\\n  -e letsencrypt_staging=false\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The playbook requires your Ansible Vault password (`--ask-vault-pass`) to decrypt `vault_rfc2136_tsig_secret` or `vault_powerdns_api_key` in [`playbooks/group_vars/all/vault.yml`](file:///home/koby/.ansible/playbooks/group_vars/all/vault.yml).","thinking":"**Analyzing Certificate Issuance Playbook**\n\nThe existence of a vault file suggests possible credential management within the project. Focus shifted to the user's question about a certificate issuing playbook. The objective is to understand how the identified playbook, `issue-deploy-certs.yml`, addresses the user's inquiry.\n\n\n**Evaluating Certificate Issuance Method**\n\nThe playbook resides on the control machine and utilizes DNS-01 challenges, resolving the earlier HTTP-01 challenge failure due to reverse proxy configuration. This DNS-01 approach avoids the need for incoming HTTP traffic, generating a TXT record for validation. Subsequently, certificates are obtained locally, copied to the target, and the service reloaded if necessary.\n\nThe playbook supports three DNS backend methods: dynamic DNS updates via BIND, PowerDNS through a REST API, and direct zone file modifications using SSH. Determining which method is in use requires further investigation of the configuration.\n\n\n"}
